#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"title: Quickstart\n"
"description: A quickstart guide for the Shaded lightcones Qiskit addon (qiskit-addon-slc)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This guide demonstrates a minimal working example of the `qiskit-addon-slc` "
"package. It calculates a shaded lightcone to reduce the sampling cost of "
"probabilistic error cancellation (PEC).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"PEC mitigates gate noise by sampling from a quasiprobability decomposition "
"of the inverse noise channel. Its sampling cost grows with every error term "
"it must mitigate; however, not every error affects the observable equally. "
"An error outside the observable's causal lightcone cannot influence the "
"measured expectation value, and even within the lightcone, some errors are "
"more detrimental than others. A shaded lightcone quantifies this by bounding"
" the effect each Pauli error term has on the observable. Truncating the "
"error terms with the smallest effect shrinks the noise model that PEC has to"
" mitigate, reducing sampling cost in exchange for a small, bounded bias.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To see how to build a realistic workflow and run on quantum hardware, check "
"out the [Probabilistic error cancellation with shaded "
"lightcones](/tutorials/pec-with-shaded-lightcones) tutorial on IBM Quantum "
"Platform.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Prepare the inputs for SLC {#1-prepare-the-inputs-for-slc}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First, build a 10-qubit Trotterized transverse-field Ising circuit and "
"choose a single-qubit $Z$ observable on the middle qubit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-addon-"
"slc/guides/quickstart/extracted-outputs/0fff4e37-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SLC operates on the noisy two-qubit-gate layers of the circuit. Here we use "
"``samplomatic`` to group the gates into annotated boxes and attach a noise-"
"injection annotation to each two-qubit layer. SLC requires every noisy box "
"to have a unique `modifier_ref`, which "
"`inject_noise_strategy=\"individual_modification\"` provides; with "
"samplomatic's default strategy, the bounds come out wrong or their "
"computation never finishes. `generate_noise_model_paulis` then enumerates "
"the Pauli error terms of every unique noisy layer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Compute the shaded lightcone {#2-compute-the-shaded-lightcone}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The shaded lightcone is assembled by assigning a scale to each Pauli error "
"term in the noise model based on how much effect it has on the observable "
"expectation value. These scales are derived from each error term's forward "
"and backward error bounds (described below), as well as its error rate:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`compute_forward_bounds` evolves each error term *forward* to the end of the"
" circuit to bound its effect on the observable measured there.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`compute_backward_bounds` evolves each error term *backward* to the start of"
" the circuit to bound its effect on the initial state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`merge_bounds` combines the two into a single bound per error term. Each "
"bound is scaled by the term's error rate. These rates typically come from a "
"noise learning experiment (such as `NoiseLearnerV3`); this guide uses random"
" rates for simplicity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the shaded lightcone visualization below, each box is shaded by how "
"strongly the errors at that site can affect the observable.  Bright boxes "
"carry the largest bounds, while boxes that fade to the background contain "
"error terms that have little effect on the calculation -- these errors are "
"natural candidates to drop from the noise model. The values in the "
"visualization below represent sums of the error bounds of all Pauli errors "
"at that site, which is why some of the values grow larger than ``2.0``, the "
"bound for any single Pauli error.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-addon-"
"slc/guides/quickstart/extracted-outputs/a2201cec-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. Reduce the sampling cost {#3-reduce-the-sampling-cost}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`compute_local_scales` turns the shaded lightcone into a concrete PEC "
"configuration. It prioritizes error terms by their bounded effect on the "
"observable and truncates the least impactful ones until the requested "
"`bias_tolerance` is reached. It returns scales for each error term: `0.0` "
"for terms to ignore during mitigation and `-1.0` for terms that should be "
"mitigated. The function also returns the resulting **sampling cost** "
"overhead ($\\gamma^2$) and a bound on the **residual bias** incurred by the "
"truncation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Setting `bias_tolerance=0.0` mitigates every error term within the causal "
"lightcone of the observable and gives a baseline sampling cost. Allowing a "
"small bias lets SLC drop the low-impact terms and reduce that cost "
"further.\n"
msgstr ""
